r college about  the Holocaust and indeed even if I had  been interested in it I couldn't because  there was no college courses almost  anywhere in the United States in the  1960s about the Holocaust when I was  growing up the dominant issue was  segregation and my earliest memory of  confronting injustice had nothing to do  with the Holocaust it was a test I  failed as a teenager when I couldn't  break with convention and offer an  elderly black lady ladened with shopping  bags my seat in the white only section  of Atlanta bus my lack of recognition of  the Holocaust and the survivors in  effect mirrored the worlds as well  the fate of Hitler's civilian victims  Jews Slavs poles Jehovah's Witnesses  gypsies was sealed in 1938 at the Evian  conference when the great democracies of  the world refused to lift their  immigration quotas and open their doors  when there was still time during the war  there were no ringing declarations from  any Allied leader of the consequences  that would flow from the genocide they  all too well knew was occurring indeed  decades later I met a wonderful man who  only recently passed away Yann Karski a  brave Polish diplomat who twice risked  his life going into the warsaw ghetto to  bear witness and then told his story  directly to President Roosevelt in the  Oval Office and as course he told me he  said that FDR listened and passively to  his story and then sent him to his  trusted confidant Supreme Court justice  Felix Frankfurter after hearing Karski  out the great jurist paused for a moment  and said sir  I'm not saying you're lying I simply  choose not to believe you after the war  the fate of survivors was little better  the British kept 52,000 survivors trying  to enter Palestine in squalid camps in  Cyprus for years those who had the  temerity to try to return home and  reclaim their homes and properties found  them occupied and they themselves were  driven off or in the worst case even  murdered hundreds of thousands of  state